The SearXNG stack is a git checkout of `github.com/chan4lk/searxng-stack`. To
update it: `cd ~/searxng-stack && git pull && ./searxng.sh restart`. Its `.env`
holds the instance secret and is git-ignored, so never commit it.

### Splash

Splash serves **one model at a time**. Both of these are downloaded:

| Model | Size on disk |
|---|---|
| `incoai/Qwen3.8-27B-Splash` | 16 GB, dense |
| `incoai/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-Splash` | 20 GB, mixture of experts |

A request that names the model that isn't loaded gets `HTTP 404 model_not_found`.

It isn't supervised by anything: it doesn't survive a reboot, and killing it
takes the model offline for every client: the MacBook's `claude-splash` and
`dsh-splash`, and OpenViking's summaries and memory extraction. **Ask the user
before stopping, restarting or switching Splash's model.**

Check what's loaded (no SSH needed) and how it was started:

```bash
curl -s http://__SERVER_IP__:8000/v1/models
studio.sh run 'ps -o command= -p $(lsof -t -iTCP:8000 -sTCP:LISTEN)'
```

**`--host` matters.** Splash defaults to `127.0.0.1`, which the MacBook can't
reach. It must run with `--host __SERVER_IP__` to be reachable over the
tailnet (`--host __SERVER_NAME__` fails: `cannot bind __SERVER_NAME__:8000`). To start it
or switch model (`MODEL` is one of the two above):

```bash
studio.sh run 'pkill -f "splash serve|libexec/server/server.py"; sleep 3; nohup splash serve --host __SERVER_IP__ --port 8000 --model MODEL --max-cache-disk 40G > ~/splash.log 2>&1 &'
```

Splash is 1.1.0 (`brew upgrade splash`; the tap only refreshes after `brew update`).
`--max-cache-disk 40G` keeps evicted prompt caches on the SSD; context defaults
to 256K. Loading takes ~10 s; check `/v1/models` until it lists the model.
After a switch, clients that name a model must match it. dsh and OpenViking
(`vlm.model` in `~/.openviking/ov.conf`) name 3.8-27B.

Splash has no `/v1/embeddings` (404): it serves chat only (`/v1/chat/completions`,
`/v1/responses`, `/v1/messages`). Use Ollama for embeddings.

`~/splash.log` has one line per request, `input … cached … tools N · TTFT`,
which is the quickest way to see why a client is slow. A cold 85K-token prompt
takes about 2 minutes before the first output token.

### claude-splash, opencode-splash, pi-splash (MacBook)

Shell functions in `~/.claude/splash/claude-splash.zsh`, sourced from
`~/.zshrc`. They are not aliases: an `alias claude-splash=` line would override
them. All three take the same leading arguments:

```bash
claude-splash [args]                  # uses whatever Splash has loaded
claude-splash 3.6|3.8 [args]          # refuses if a different model is loaded
claude-splash --switch 3.6|3.8 [args] # asks, restarts Splash with it, waits, launches
```

- **Models:** it reads `/v1/models` and points every model setting (main,
  Opus/Sonnet/Haiku defaults, subagents) at the loaded model, through a
  per-run copy of `~/.claude/splash-settings.json`. That file also sets
  `CLAUDE_CODE_MAX_CONTEXT_TOKENS=131072`.
- **No MCP:** it launches with `--strict-mcp-config` and
  `ENABLE_CLAUDEAI_MCP_SERVERS=false`. The user's MCP servers and claude.ai
  connectors add about 60K prompt tokens and 180 tools. With them, the first
  reply took 4 minutes; without them it takes 25–35 s, and later turns reuse
  Splash's cache and start in under a second.
- **Prompt cache:** it also sets `CLAUDE_CODE_ATTRIBUTION_HEADER=0`,
  `CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_GIT_INSTRUCTIONS=1`, `CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_EXPERIMENTAL_BETAS=1`
  and `--exclude-dynamic-system-prompt-sections`. Without that flag every new
  session missed Splash's cache (`cached 0`, ~25 s); with it a new session
  reuses the 23K-token prefix (~0.4 s to first token).
- **Qwen3.6 profile:** when the 35B-A3B is loaded, `claude-splash` adds
  harness-side help for the weaker model. `SPLASH_PROFILE=off` skips it, for
  A/B runs; `SPLASH_PROFILE=force` applies it to any model.
  - `splash-proxy.py`, a per-run local proxy, adds Qwen's coding sampling
    (temperature 0.6, top_p 0.95, top_k 20) and
    `context_management: clear_thinking keep:all`. That's the only way Splash's
    Anthropic API turns on `preserve_thinking`; without it Qwen3.6 sends
    empty-argument tool calls after a few turns.
  - A `PostToolUse` hook (`hooks/check-edit.sh`) syntax/lint-checks each
    edited file. It exits 2 so the model fixes errors at once.
  - A `Stop` hook (`hooks/verify-on-stop.sh`) blocks finishing while the
    project's verify command fails. It's opt-in per repo:
    `.claude/splash-verify` holds one command line, or set `SPLASH_VERIFY_CMD`.
    It blocks at most 3 times per session.
  - `qwen36-discipline.md` is appended to the system prompt (plan, check
    every edit, loop guard, no "done" without output).
  - It also sets `CLAUDE_CODE_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS=32768`, autocompact at 60%
    and `BASH_MAX_OUTPUT_LENGTH=15000`.
  - These files live in `~/.claude/splash/` and come from
    `clients/claude-code/splash/` in the repo.
- **opencode-splash** runs OpenCode from its own lean config in
  `~/.config/opencode-splash/opencode/opencode.json`: Splash provider only, no
  plugins, `skill` and `webfetch` tools off. Sessions and auth stay shared with
  normal opencode. The user's normal config loads oh-my-openagent (Sisyphus),
  which sent 80K tokens and 46 tools with no cross-session cache (~104 s first
  token), and its `small_model` points at an offline MacBook server. The lean
  config sends ~10K tokens and 8 tools, ~6 s per reply. In scripts, give
  `opencode run` `</dev/null`, or it waits on stdin.
- **pi-splash** runs Pi with the `splash` provider in `~/.pi/agent/models.json`:
  4 tools, ~14K tokens.
- **Adding a model:** edit the `_SPLASH_MODELS` table at the top of the file,
  plus the provider model lists in the OpenCode and Pi configs.

### dsh (MacBook)

| Command | What it runs |
|---|---|
| `dsh-splash` | dsh on Splash, web UI by default (profile `web`) |
| `dsh-splash --profile headless "task"` | One-shot task |

The `web` profile has these plugins: dshmarket (Settings → Plugin Market),
dsh-better-sidebar@0.19.1, dsh-chat-import, dsh-agent-teams@0.1.22 and
@openviking/dsh-memory-plugin. The `dsh-tui` npm plugin doesn't work with dsh
0.1.5, which is installed: it crashes with a blank screen. dsh-tvision worked
but was removed (the user found it ugly); use `opencode-splash` or `pi-splash`
for a terminal UI.

- **Plugin versions:** pin ones built for dsh 0.1.5.
- **Installing:** `dsh plugin add` can fail with
  `No matching version found for @deepseek-ai/dsh-settings@>=0.1.5 <0.2.0-0`.
- **The web profile reloads live:** writing a bundle into `package.json` before
  its package is installed breaks the running instance.
- **Safe route:** install into a copy of the profile (e.g. `web-stage`) and
  boot it on another port (`--port 18765 --no-open`). Then copy its
  `pnpm-lock.yaml` and dependencies into the real profile, run
  `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`, and add to `dsh.profile.bundles` last.

### OpenViking (agent memory)

OpenViking 0.4.22 runs from a uv venv at `~/openviking/.venv`, with its `ov`
CLI at `~/openviking/.venv/bin/ov`. It keeps data in `~/.openviking/data` and
logs to `~/Library/Logs/openviking.log`. It uses ~450 MB of RAM.

- **Config:** `~/.openviking/ov.conf` (chmod 600, holds `server.root_api_key`).
  It binds 127.0.0.1:1933 in `api_key` auth mode, with account and user
  `chandima`.
- **Embeddings:** Ollama `qwen3-embedding:0.6b-8k`, 1024 dimensions. It's a
  custom 8K-context variant of `qwen3-embedding:0.6b` that uses 2.9 GB instead
  of 5.8 GB and unloads after 5 idle minutes. It goes through the `openai`
  provider at `http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1`, with `encoding_format: float`.
- **LLM:** Splash, `incoai/Qwen3.8-27B-Splash`, with
  `extra_request_body: {"reasoning_effort": "none"}` and a 900 s timeout.
  Summaries and memory extraction queue behind chat turns. They 404 while
  another Splash model is loaded.
- **Changing the embedding model name** fails at startup with
  `EmbeddingRebuildRequiredError`. If the vectors are identical, start once
  with `embedding.allow_metadata_override=true`, then remove it.
- **Managing it:** `launchctl kickstart -k gui/$(id -u)/com.openviking.server`
  restarts it, `launchctl print …` shows status (ask before `bootout`), and
  `~/openviking/.venv/bin/openviking-server doctor` checks the config.
- **Clients:** the MacBook's `~/.openviking/ovcli.conf` (chmod 600) holds
  `url`, the user `api_key`, `account` and `user`, but no root key. The dsh
  memory plugin reads it; `OPENVIKING_URL`, `OPENVIKING_API_KEY`,
  `OPENVIKING_ACCOUNT` and `OPENVIKING_USER` override it.
- **Home alias:** `viking://~/` resolves to `viking://user/chandima`.

### image-server

When idle it uses about 100 MB. The first request loads the model, which takes
a minute or more. It unloads after 10 idle minutes (`IMAGE_IDLE_UNLOAD`). Only
one variant is loaded at a time: `edit` serves generations, transparent output
and edits; `img2img` loads only for `image` + `strength` variations and swaps
`edit` out. Requests run one at a time, and after 4 are waiting, new ones get
`429`. mflux is pinned to commit acdfc98 (reference editing isn't in a release
yet).

| Task | Endpoint | Body |
|---|---|---|
| Generate | `/v1/images/generations` | `prompt`, `size` (multiples of 32), `steps`, `seed`, `transparent` |
| Variation | `/v1/images/generations` | plus `image` (base64/data URL), `strength` 0.05–0.95 (higher = more change) |
| Edit / merge / restyle | `/v1/images/edits` | `images` (1–10, base64/data URL), `prompt` naming "image 1", "image 2"…, `output_resolution` (512 is fast), optional `size` |

At 512×512 in 8-bit: generate ~20 s, 2-image merge ~47 s, restyle ~12 s,
transparent ~37 s. For a style change, `edit` with "repaint image 1 as …" works
far better than `strength`, which barely restyles.

**Before `restart`, `stop` or `install`**, check `/health`. The script refuses
while `busy` or `queued` is set, because a restart cuts off whoever is
generating. Use `FORCE=1` only if the user agrees.

**Memory guard: off** (`IMAGE_MEMORY_GUARD=0`, at the user's request; it
refused too many jobs). Requests load the quantization they ask for (default
8-bit) and run; macOS swaps if memory is tight. Measured 8-bit peaks: ~21 GB
at 512², ~25 GB at 768², ~29.5 GB at 1024², ~33 GB for img2img. Avoid bf16
(~46 GB) while Splash is running. `/health` → `memory` shows the breakdown
and swap pressure. To re-enable the guard: `IMAGE_MEMORY_GUARD=1
./image-server.sh install`. Free the image model's memory immediately with
`image-server.sh unload`. The service
writes PNGs to `~/image-server-outputs` and deletes them after 7 days.

**From dsh:** the plugin `~/.dsh/plugins/image-generate.mjs` adds two tools:
- `generate_image`: `prompt`, `size`, `steps`, `transparent`, and
  `init_image` + `strength` for variations.
- `edit_image`: `images` (1–10 workspace paths), `prompt`, `detail`
  (low/medium/high), and optional `size`.

Both save PNGs into the session's workspace (default `generated-images/`, or a
`filename` relative to the workspace), and refuse any input or output path
outside it. They find the server through `config.url` on their entry in
`~/.dsh/splash.patch.yml`, which `$IMAGE_SERVER_URL` overrides; `searxng-search`
works the same way. If a tool reports `unreachable at http://127.0.0.1:…`,
neither value reached it: re-run `./install.sh` and restart dsh.

The chat UI only draws images on `read_image`'s own card, so when the current
model accepts images, the result tells the agent to call `read_image` on the
saved file. Only the 35B-A3B entry declares image input (`input: [text, image]`
in `splash.settings.yaml`); on the 27B the image is saved but not shown.

### music-server

It supervises ACE-Step 1.5's own API server (`~/ace-step`, pinned commit
ca1e85f, models in `~/ace-step/checkpoints`). The first request starts
`acestep-api` on `127.0.0.1:8001`, with the MLX LM backend and the
`acestep-5Hz-lm-1.7B` planner. After 10 idle minutes it stops that process
entirely, which frees all its memory. When idle the service uses ~50 MB.
ACE-Step is MIT-licensed, so the output is fine for the user's business
content; don't swap in YuE2 (CC-BY-NC). The backend log is
`~/Library/Logs/music-backend.log`. From dsh, the `generate_music` tool saves
into `generated-music/`.

### Malformed tool calls (dsh)

Splash's model often fuses argument names with their values or with each
other in dsh (`formatmp3`, `bpm85`, `instrumentaltrue`,
`"filename audio/x.mp3": 20`), sometimes on the first call of a session.
The media tools share `~/.dsh/plugins/tool-args.mjs`, which splits and
validates those keys. The first malformed call in a session is rejected with
the corrected JSON to resend; later ones go ahead with the repaired arguments
and a `Note:` in the result. If an agent loops anyway, start a fresh session:
broken calls left in the history get copied. More memory won't help.

### studio-guard (dsh)

`~/.dsh/plugins/studio-guard.mjs` denies any dsh tool call that reaches this
server (`ssh`, `scp` or `tailscale ssh` to its IP or name, or `studio.sh run`)
and contains a stop, kill or restart verb: `kill`/`pkill`/`killall`, `launchctl`
bootout/stop, `shutdown`/`reboot`, `colima stop`, `docker … down/stop/kill/rm`,
`image-server.sh stop/restart/unload`, `searxng.sh down`, `tailscale down`,
`rm -r`, or `/v1/unload`. Read-only checks pass. It was added after a dsh agent
ran `pkill -f splash` to free memory for an image job. It covers dsh agents
only: in Claude Code, the 'ask first' rules below still apply.

## Common mistakes

- **Freeing memory by stopping something.** An image-server 503 means: report the numbers to the user and stop. Never kill Splash or the user's own jobs (e.g. an OCR `llama-server`) to make room.
- **Deleting or overwriting files, or stopping services, without asking.** The Studio is shared infrastructure, so confirm these first. Read-only checks such as `health`, `status` and `logs` are fine without asking.
- **Trusting `brew services list`.** It can show Colima as `stopped` while the VM is running. Check with `colima status`.
- **Installing packages.** Use `HOMEBREW_NO_AUTO_UPDATE=1 NONINTERACTIVE=1 brew install …`. A `brew install` that waits for input hangs the SSH session.
- **Opening SearXNG to the internet.** Its bot limiter is off because it's reached only from the tailnet. Never bind it to `0.0.0.0` or enable `tailscale funnel`. The same goes for OpenViking.
- **Blaming the MacBook's DNS when a `…ts.net` URL fails.** Check the Studio's current name first: `tailscale status --json` → the peer's `DNSName`. See Machine.
- **Editing this skill, `studio.sh`, `splash-settings.json` or `claude-splash.zsh` in place only.** `~/searxng-stack/install.sh` renders them from templates in that repo. Update the matching `.tmpl` too, and check that `./install.sh --dry-run` reports them as unchanged.
